South Africa, like many other countries, is experiencing a number of rapid environmental changes that are likely to have major impacts on its terrestrial, freshwater and marine ecosystems. These changes include increases in temperature (as a result of global climate change), changes in the frequency, timing and severity of rainfall, as well as increased ultraviolet radiation. In addition, a number of biological invasions are occurring simultaneously.
The potential impacts of these changes on South Africa’s ecosystems have been identified as a high priority research area by the South African National Research Foundation (NRF). To address this research priority, the NRF has funded a number of research programmes, including a national programme on “Biological Invasions as a Component of South Africa’s Global Change Research Effort”. This programme is a collaboration between a number of South African institutions and is co-ordinated by Professor Mike Coughlan of Rhodes University, with co-investigators Professor Melodie McGeoch of the University of Cape Town and Professor Peter Williams of Rhodes University.

The overall goal of the programme is to determine the impacts of environmental change, with biological invasions as one component, on South Africa’s ecosystems. The programme is divided into a number of integrated projects, each of which addresses one, or a combination of these five themes:

  1. Modelling the potential impacts of global environmental change on South Africa’s ecosystems;
  2. Assessing the risks to South Africa’s biodiversity and ecosystem functioning posed by biological invasions;
  3. Identifying and prioritising biological invasions that pose a threat to South Africa’s ecosystems;
  4. Developing risk management tools to aid decision making in managing biological invasions;
  5. Modelling the potential spread of four invasive alien tree species on mountain slopes in South Africa.

The five projects are being undertaken by a multidisciplinary team of researchers comprising demographers, climatologists, geographers, ecologists, biogeochemists, hydrologists, hydroecologists, mathematicians, biologists, palaeoecologists and entomologists, and are all based in South African institutions. The team is working together to integrate data and models across spatial and temporal scales, from local to global, and from months to millennia, respectively.
