It is estimated that 200 million girls and women around the world have undergone female genital mutilation/cutting (FGM/C).  (The terms FGM and cutting are used interchangeably.) Every year, an estimated three million new cases occur.  The practice is most common in Africa, but it also exists in the Middle East and in Asian, Latin American, North American and Australian communities.  FGM/C is largely found in Muslim and African communities, but also occurs in Christian, Jewish and other communities.  The practice is becoming less common in some countries in Africa, but is increasing in other countries in the world.  In the United States, for example, the number of cases is growing, especially among Somali immigrants.  In Australia, the practice is increasing among young girls born outside the country to Somali, Sudanese, Egyptian, Ethiopian, Eritrean and Indonesian parents.  In refugee camps, FGM/C is often performed on young girls by people who have little or no medical training.  In some places, girls who have undergone FGM/C are not allowed to attend school or are removed from school if they are found to have undergone the procedure.  In some places, the practice is performed on infants as young as four days old.  In some places, girls who have not undergone the procedure are not allowed to marry.  In some places, a girl who has not undergone the procedure is considered unclean.

FGM/C includes all procedures that involve the partial or total removal of the external female genitalia, or other injury to the female genital organs for non-medical purposes.  The three most common types are:

  • Type I: excision of the clitoris (clitoridectomy);
  • Type II: excision of the clitoris with the labia minora;
  • Type III: excision of the clitoris with the labia minora, labia majora, and often the opening of the vagina (the vulva or perineal wound).

Other procedures, such as piercing, incision, scraping and cauterizing the genital area are also used.  In the most severe cases, girls' breasts and/or legs may be cut or chopped off.  In some places, a mixture of herbs and oils is inserted into the vagina.  In some places, the vaginal and/or vaginal opening is sewn up, leaving a small hole for urine and menstrual blood to pass through.  This type of procedure is extremely painful and can cause death due to infection
